WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.628
Start with why, because
every design choice

2
00:00:02.628 --> 00:00:05.350
in the next forty-five
minutes follows from it.

3
00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:10.909
OMB M-22-09 says federal applications
can't rely on the network perimeter,

4
00:00:10.909 --> 00:00:14.432
and that every request for
access should be evaluated,

5
00:00:14.432 --> 00:00:18.425
with the ability to evaluate an
active session continuously.

6
00:00:18.425 --> 00:00:20.115
When undue risk appears,

7
00:00:20.115 --> 00:00:24.425
the answer can be re-authentication,
limited access or denial.

8
00:00:24.425 --> 00:00:28.784
It also asks agencies to move past
static roles toward more granular,

9
00:00:28.784 --> 00:00:34.138
dynamic permissions, and to use at least
one device-level signal alongside identity.

10
00:00:34.138 --> 00:00:38.675
NIST SP 800-207 gives the architecture.

11
00:00:38.675 --> 00:00:43.996
Three of its tenets carry this book:
network location alone doesn't imply trust;

12
00:00:43.996 --> 00:00:47.515
access is granted per session,
with least privilege;

13
00:00:47.515 --> 00:00:50.462
and access is determined
by dynamic policy.

14
00:00:50.462 --> 00:00:53.049
It also separates deciding from enforcing.

15
00:00:53.049 --> 00:00:57.728
A policy decision point, made of a policy
engine and a policy administrator,

16
00:00:57.728 --> 00:01:01.425
decides; policy enforcement
points carry the decision out.

17
00:01:01.425 --> 00:01:04.787
The table is how the program
maps that onto Entra ID.

18
00:01:04.787 --> 00:01:07.337
Conditional Access is the policy engine.

19
00:01:07.337 --> 00:01:11.505
Entra ID's token issuance
plays the policy administrator:

20
00:01:11.505 --> 00:01:15.325
it issues the token the
decision allows, or it refuses.

21
00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:18.764
The applications that accept
only Entra-issued tokens,

22
00:01:18.764 --> 00:01:23.475
and the gateways in front of older
applications, are the enforcement points.

23
00:01:23.475 --> 00:01:29.475
And Continuous Access Evaluation answers
M-22-09's active-session requirement,

24
00:01:29.475 --> 00:01:33.370
re-evaluating on critical events,
such as a disabled account,

25
00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.500
a password reset or token revocation,
and when location changes.

26
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.925
Be careful how you state that
mapping in a security plan.

27
00:01:40.925 --> 00:01:45.875
It's the program's interpretation, not
a mapping Microsoft or NIST publishes.

28
00:01:45.875 --> 00:01:51.269
But it explains why the rest of this session
treats a Conditional Access policy as

29
00:01:51.269 --> 00:01:53.013
the unit of access control:

30
00:01:53.013 --> 00:01:56.710
one policy set the agency can
read, test, change and prove.
